(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-dermatological-infestations-vignettes"]={"title":"Dermatological Infestations Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 52-year-old man living in a shelter has intense itching with linear excoriations across his back, neck, shoulders and waist, and patches of darker pigmentation where older lesions have healed. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Scabies of the trunk and back","Body louse infestation","Head louse infestation","Pubic louse infestation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — that distribution with post-inflammatory pigmentation in chronic cases is characteristic. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 6-year-old boy has suspected Rocky Mountain spotted fever. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Doxycycline at 2.2 mg per kilogram by mouth twice daily for five to ten days","Amoxicillin at a weight-based dose by mouth for a course of fourteen days","Azithromycin at a weight-based dose by mouth for a course of five days","Intravenous ceftriaxone at a weight-based dose for a course of ten days"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is used in children and pregnancy here, with desensitisation where contraindicated. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has suspected scabies with several unexcoriated papules on the wrist. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Punch biopsy of an active lesion sent for direct immunofluorescence studies","Potassium hydroxide preparation of a plucked hair examined under a microscope","Skin scraping of an unexcoriated burrow with mineral oil on a number 15 blade","Serological testing for immunoglobulin M and immunoglobulin G against the mite"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — microscopic identification of the organism, ova or faeces is the definitive diagnosis. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man treated for scabies has scrubbed his skin with a strong antibacterial soap several times a day and now has widespread irritation. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["The irritation indicates a secondary bacterial infection needing oral antibiotics","Excessive washing with harsh soap can worsen the skin irritation and should be stopped","Vigorous washing is essential to remove the mites from the surface of the skin","The irritation confirms the treatment has failed and a second agent is needed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the permethrin course itself is what treats the infestation. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 9-year-old boy has had head lice treated twice without success. Which is the most appropriate explanation to give the parents?","choices":["Nits remaining in the hair after treatment prove the infestation is still active","Resistance is increasing, so a multimodal approach combining physical and chemical methods is warranted","Failure means the diagnosis is wrong, so the itching is more likely to be seborrhoeic dermatitis","Occlusive home remedies such as mayonnaise or mineral oil are the recommended next step"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — pediculicidal effect is read twenty-four hours after application. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man treated for hookworm folliculitis has persistent follicular pustules after one course of albendazole. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Apply cryotherapy to each pustule, since freezing kills the larvae","Repeat the course, since hookworm folliculitis may need repeated treatments","Switch to topical therapy, which is more effective for the follicular form","Excise the affected follicles, since surgery clears resistant lesions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the folliculitic form is specifically noted to be more resistant. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 42-year-old woman bitten by a hobo spider has fatigue, headache and difficulty concentrating a week later. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Headaches may persist for about a week and the symptoms are managed supportively","These symptoms indicate an evolving necrotic wound needing surgical review","These symptoms indicate a secondary infection requiring an oral antibiotic","These symptoms indicate an allergic reaction needing an antihistamine course"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — death from severe systemic effects, including aplastic anaemia, is rare. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man is travelling to Nigeria and asks how to avoid tungiasis. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Use DEET-based repellent reapplied every two hours and perform daily body checks","Avoid contact with the bedding and underclothing of anyone who is infested","Inspect hotel headboards and picture frames before unpacking any luggage","Do not walk barefoot or in sandals on beaches, and do not sit directly on the sand"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — Nigeria, the Caribbean, India and Brazil are the named endemic settings. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 6-year-old girl has been treated for head lice and her mother asks what to do about the house. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Wash everything at 60 degrees Celsius and treat every household member immediately","Engage a professional exterminator to treat cracks and crevices around the beds","Put clothing and bedding from the past week in the dryer or bag it for two weeks, wash combs and vacuum","Have the home professionally fumigated and then discard all the pillows and the soft furnishings"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — fumigation is specifically not recommended. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman returned from a hotel stay and has itchy wheals in rows of three across her forearms and shoulders. She did not feel any bites, and there are small blood flecks on her sheets. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Papular urticaria from a caterpillar","Bedbug bites","Flea bites from the family Pulicidae","Scabies acquired from hotel bedding"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — painless bites grouped in a linear row of three, the breakfast, lunch and dinner sign. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman returned from the Caribbean where she lay on the sand. She has an intensely itchy, raised, winding red track on her foot that has moved about 2 cm since yesterday. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["A scabies burrow of the foot","Cercarial dermatitis after swimming","Cutaneous larva migrans","Tungiasis of the plantar surface"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a serpentine trail advancing 2 to 3 cm daily, from animal hookworm larvae in contaminated sand. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 14-year-old boy swam in a Michigan lake yesterday. He felt prickling on his legs for about half an hour afterwards, then severe itching by bedtime, and today has red papules turning into small blisters. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cutaneous larva migrans from the sand at the shore","Flea bites acquired at the lakeside from an animal","Cercarial dermatitis, also called swimmer's itch","Contact dermatitis from something in the lake water"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — prickling for about 30 minutes, itch at 10 to 12 hours, papules within 24 hours. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man with a black widow spider bite is being treated for envenomation. Which additional measure should be checked?","choices":["That his tetanus vaccination is up to date","That he has had a course of prophylactic antibiotics","That an antivenom skin test has been performed first","That the wound has been debrided before he is admitted"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside calcium gluconate, narcotics, muscle relaxants and benzodiazepines. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman finds an attached tick on her leg after gardening in Maine. Which is the most appropriate immediate action?","choices":["Leave the tick in place until it detaches, since removal can force in more organism","Start a ten to fourteen day course of doxycycline the same day as the bite","Send serology the same day and treat only if the result comes back positive","Remove the tick immediately, then watch for the rash and constitutional symptoms"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — antibiotics are indicated once the disease is diagnosed, at all stages. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with suspected Rocky Mountain spotted fever is on day 4 of illness. The indirect immunofluorescence assay has been sent. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Wait for the assay result, since the gold standard test should guide the choice of antibiotic","Start amoxicillin now, since doxycycline should be reserved for confirmed disease","Repeat the assay in three days and treat only if it has become positive by then","Start doxycycline now, since treatment should begin by day 5 and the assay is rarely diagnostic before day 7"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — treatment must not wait for the serology to return. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman stung by a wasp develops generalised urticaria, lip swelling and wheeze within ten minutes. Which is the most appropriate immediate treatment?","choices":["Calcium gluconate with a muscle relaxant and a benzodiazepine given intravenously","Epinephrine, given intramuscularly or subcutaneously, with emergency transfer and supportive measures","An oral antihistamine with a short course of prednisolone and review the following day","Ice to the sting site with injection of a local anaesthetic for control of the pain"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — this is an anaphylactic reaction rather than a severe local one. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 10-year-old boy is stung by a honeybee and the stinger is still visible in the skin. Which is the most appropriate immediate action?","choices":["Scrape it off with a card edge held parallel to the skin, as quickly as possible","Grasp it with fine forceps and withdraw it with steady traction along its axis","Apply ice for twenty minutes and remove it once the swelling has settled down","Leave it in place, since removal releases the remaining venom into the tissue"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the barbed ovipositor keeps pumping venom while it remains impaled. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman in Connecticut removed a tick from her thigh eight days ago. She now has a 9 cm round red patch there with a paler centre and a darker dot in the middle, plus fever and aching. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Stage 1 Lyme disease with erythema migrans","Cellulitis at the site of the tick bite","A local hypersensitivity reaction to tick saliva","Early Rocky Mountain spotted fever"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a lesion over 5 cm with central clearing about a week after the bite, with constitutional symptoms. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman with suspected scabies has heavily excoriated hands and no intact burrow visible. Which test is most appropriate?","choices":["A potassium hydroxide wet mount of a plucked hair examined under the microscope","Serology for immunoglobulin M and immunoglobulin G against the causative mite","A bacterial culture taken from an excoriated area and sent for sensitivities","The burrow ink test, looking for a zigzag line running across and away from the lesion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — blue or black ink is applied to a suspected lesion when scraping is difficult. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man bitten by a brown recluse two days ago has a darkening area at the site plus nausea, headache and chills. Which is the most appropriate interpretation?","choices":["Systemic symptoms mean the wound requires wide surgical excision within the next day","Systemic symptoms develop one to two days after the bite and necrosis at two to three days","Systemic symptoms indicate secondary bacterial infection and need immediate antibiotics","Systemic symptoms this early exclude a brown recluse and suggest a black widow bite"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — eschar forms between days five and seven, then deep ulcers. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has a classic erythema migrans lesion after a tick bite in Vermont. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Send a Western blot and treat only if the immunoglobulin G band is positive","Reassure and review in two weeks, since most tick bites need no treatment","Diagnose and treat him on clinical grounds without waiting for serology","Send an enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay and withhold treatment until it returns"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a patient with the lesion is treated clinically, and testing is most useful outside endemic regions. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man with cutaneous larva migrans of the foot asks about treatment. Which is the most appropriate answer?","choices":["Cryotherapy applied to the advancing end of the track where the larva is located","Surgical excision of the leading edge of the lesion under local anaesthetic","Topical permethrin to the affected area with a second application at one week","Albendazole 400 mg by mouth daily for three days, or ivermectin for one to two days"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — topical therapy is less effective and surgery or cryotherapy is not recommended. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 16-year-old girl with cercarial dermatitis has pain and swelling with the itch, worst about two days after exposure. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Antihistamines, oatmeal baths, antipruritic lotions, aspirin for pain and topical or oral glucocorticoids","Albendazole 400 mg by mouth daily for three days to clear the parasite from the skin","Overnight topical permethrin applied to the whole skin surface with a repeat dose at one week","Oral doxycycline for ten days to cover the organism carried in the fresh water"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the pain and swelling peak at 48 to 72 hours before settling. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"A 9-year-old boy has caterpillar dermatitis with visible fine hairs still on the skin. Which physical measure is most appropriate?","choices":["Strip the hairs off with adhesive tape","Scrape the area with a card edge held flat","Comb the area after applying hair moisturiser","Apply blue ink and look for a zigzag line"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside antihistamines, menthol or camphor, and topical corticosteroid. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman has a swollen painful knee that has come and gone over two years, plus memory difficulty and low mood. She recalls a rash after a tick bite three years ago that was never treated. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Chronic Rocky Mountain spotted fever sequelae","Stage 3 Lyme disease, late persistent infection","Stage 2 Lyme disease, early disseminated infection","Rheumatoid arthritis with an unrelated mood disorder"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — monoarticular arthritis of a weight-bearing joint with subacute encephalopathy. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man stepped on a fire ant mound and has flushing, widespread hives, abdominal pain and vomiting. Which mechanism explains these features?","choices":["The venom contains the neurotoxin alpha-latrotoxin","The venom is cytotoxic and causes local necrosis","The hairs cause mechanical irritation of the skin","The venom induces mast cell degranulation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — fire ants usually attack in groups, which increases the venom load. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man has an eruption whose reaction pattern has been recognised. Which characteristics are used next to narrow the differential?","choices":["Duration of the eruption, the patient's age, and whether any treatment has been tried so far","Culture and sensitivity of the lesion together with a complete blood count and chemistry","Presence of pruritus, presence of pain, and whether regional lymph nodes are enlarged","Colour, shape, configuration of the lesions relative to one another, and their distribution on the body"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — those tune the differential the reaction pattern has already generated. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman and the three other members of her household are being treated for scabies. Which household measure is required?","choices":["Vacuum floors, carpets, upholstery and play areas, but avoid any fumigation of the home","Discard all bedding and soft furnishings used in the week before treatment was started","Wash bedding and clothing at 60 degrees Celsius, or bag it in a warm place for fourteen days","Engage a professional exterminator, since the mite survives in cracks and crevices"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — high heat is needed to kill both mites and ova, and all infested persons are treated. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man bitten by a brown recluse six days ago now has a black eschar over the site with a deep ulcer forming beneath it. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Intravenous calcium gluconate with muscle relaxants and benzodiazepines for the pain","Oral doxycycline for ten days to prevent the wound becoming secondarily infected","Pain control, warm compresses and avoiding strenuous exercise, deferring surgery until the wound is stable","Immediate wide excision of the necrotic area with primary closure of the resulting skin defect"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — necrotic wounds heal very slowly and may later need reconstruction. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman handling her pet tarantula developed a red painful eye an hour later and now has blurred vision. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Urgent ophthalmology referral, since embedded hairs can cause corneal granuloma","Topical corticosteroid to the eyelid skin with review in one week's time","Oral antihistamine with artificial tears and reassessment in three days","Irrigation of the eye at home with saline and no further follow-up needed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — topical corticosteroid addresses only the cutaneous reaction. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 66"}]};
